WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.060
Film Courage: Whether you were writing SEARCH
AND RESCUE or just in general, do you rely

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:10.940
on certain structure to help you start the
screenplay, finish the screenplay?

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.350
Jack Perez, Filmmaker: Do you mean a book
for the source material or a book for inspiration?

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:16.290
Film Courage: A writing book for inspiration.

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:20.990
Jack: Well, you know there is one book that
(I’ve probably mentioned this before) one

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:24.539
book by Stephen King On Writing [A Memoir
To The Craft: On Writing] which was key when

00:00:24.539 --> 00:00:27.350
I started to sit down and do SEARCH AND RESCUE.

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:32.700
I don’t know why because Valerie did not
point me to that book but I think somebody

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.920
introduced it to me or I just came across
it right at the point where I was like “How

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:38.110
am I going to do this?

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:39.110
Am I really going to commit?

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:40.770
Am I going to do this?”

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:44.000
And then I read that book by Stephen King
which is this really thin book by Stephen

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:45.000
King.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.000
Have you read it?

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.000
Film Courage: Yes and it’s great.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.000
And he talks about his life.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.000
It’s not just a ‘how-to-book.’

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Jack: Right.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.450
It’s not just one of these “This is how
you write a book or a screenplay.”

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.040
It was very conversational like his stuff
is.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:01:02.410
It was very unpretentious and it some how
demystified it and made it easier to start.

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:10.990
So that book was really key because I felt
like I just needed to…It just seemed less

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:17.700
complicated then reading one of these classic
screenwriting books “Where’s the inciting

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:18.700
incident?”

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:19.700
And things like that.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:20.700
It wasn’t any of that stuff.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:22.460
It was just “This is how I do it.”

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:24.369
It works for me and maybe it will work for
you?

00:01:24.369 --> 00:01:30.549
And that was key and I actually recommend
that book when people say “What’s the

00:01:30.549 --> 00:01:31.549
book?”

00:01:31.549 --> 00:01:33.020
That’s the book I recommend for writers.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:37.310
Film Courage: Yes I thought it was good too
and I forgot exactly how he says it (it’s

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:44.270
been a few years since I read it) when it
actually became a writer (he was hired) and

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:52.090
it was difficult to finish things without
substances and all of that.

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:56.520
And you wouldn’t think that this would be
a problem because all of us are waiting to

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.119
get to that level where we don’t have to
have a day job.

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:04.520
And then he talks about not having a day job
and having to be disciplined and things like

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:05.520
that.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:06.729
That’s a problem I think a lot of us don’t
think about.

00:02:06.729 --> 00:02:12.240
Jack: No, no and also there’s a pressure
now you’re not…now you are this thing,

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:19.069
you are this professional thing so you’re
are expected to deliver you know?

00:02:19.069 --> 00:02:27.909
I just think he made it easy for the prospective
writer to realize that yes this is probably

00:02:27.909 --> 00:02:34.760
in you and you just have to be serious about
it and get down to business.

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:43.790
And it’s the unofficial-ness of it or not
mathematical step-by-step process that I think

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:45.580
made the book good.

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:50.500
It spoke to me anyway because I didn’t think
that way anyway.

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:57.549
I didn’t naturally feel like I needed to
outline every single scene of the movie before

00:02:57.549 --> 00:02:58.549
I wrote it.

00:02:58.549 --> 00:03:02.829
And in a way it started to kill any of the
joy because writing is so hard.

00:03:02.829 --> 00:03:11.249
To me the discovery of the piece as you write
it as one of the most enjoyable parts of writing

00:03:11.249 --> 00:03:18.030
for me because otherwise you’re just executing
something that has already been formed and

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:21.459
to a certain degree that’s what you’re
doing when you’re making a film but there

00:03:21.459 --> 00:03:26.769
is such a translation from the word to the
image with actors and everything, it’s such

00:03:26.769 --> 00:03:29.249
a huge translation that doesn’t feel boring.

00:03:29.249 --> 00:03:33.209
Although I heard that Hitchcock used to say
that by the time I made the movie it was so

00:03:33.209 --> 00:03:37.870
designed I was bored to death because I knew
it so well that I didn’t even want to show

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:39.800
up, it was done in my head.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:46.989
But for writing I felt that it was so laid
out that I would just be a slave to this outline…now

00:03:46.989 --> 00:03:49.560
I’ve got to write this scene…now I’ve
got to write that scene.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:54.219
As opposed to something that would sort of
reveal itself.

00:03:54.219 --> 00:03:57.779
So that to me is like the coolest part of
writing is when the story starts to reveal

00:03:57.779 --> 00:03:58.779
itself.

00:03:58.779 --> 00:04:00.269
So that to me is like the coolest part of
writing when the story starts to reveal itself

00:04:00.269 --> 00:04:05.549
to you it becomes a living thing.

00:04:05.549 --> 00:04:10.319
Film Courage: Have you ever followed a strict
routine?

00:04:10.319 --> 00:04:11.969
You seem like maybe you vacillate?

00:04:11.969 --> 00:04:15.629
Jack: Yes it depends on the situation.

00:04:15.629 --> 00:04:18.840
With SEARCH AND RESCUE I knew if I didn’t
get up every day and write, I wouldn’t do

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:19.840
it.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:21.109
It would never get done.

00:04:21.109 --> 00:04:25.150
Almost did it so I could just get it over
with you know what I mean?

00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:28.430
If you just get up and do it…that’s the
math I did.

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:30.740
If I can write three pages a day, it’s finite.

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:38.810
That means if I can only have the burden of
writing three pages, after 40 days or whatever

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:41.490
it will be done at least there will be something
done.

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:44.880
So just like a diet or something just do it.

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:50.120
Nobody wants to do it but I can do it for
a month, I can actually do it for a month.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:53.580
Now that’s why (going back to the “real
writer”) a “real writer” stays on that

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:56.669
diet their whole life, they just keep doing
it.

00:04:56.669 --> 00:04:59.449
But I also think that the “real writer”
is addicted in the way that a filmmaker is.

00:04:59.449 --> 00:05:01.919
In other words it can’t just be a chore.

00:05:01.919 --> 00:05:04.800
As much as a writer will say it’s hard,
it can’t be a chore.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.720
It has to be an addiction or else you will
not (just like going to the gym for a lot

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:08.720
of people is an addiction).

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:14.620
There is something in the adrenaline, you
just get jacked up and now you need to do

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:23.169
this thing so I didn’t continue that once
I started writing this script it was like

00:05:23.169 --> 00:05:25.500
now I have to think about how I’m going
to make it.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:29.410
That’s why I don’t say I’m a classic
writer because if that was the case I would

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:32.060
just put that away and start writing the next
thing.

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:38.930
But to me it’s just the step (necessary
step) so I can now set about making the movie.

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:42.699
So that’s where I am and I went through
the writing process so I could make the movie.

00:05:42.699 --> 00:05:45.580
Film Courage: And you have a campaign on Indiegogo.

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:50.990
Jack: Yes, we have an Indiegogo campaign,
it’s continuing until the end of October

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:51.990
[2018].

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:59.610
And everything is one there…that was another
thing it was like what does it take to make

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:01.419
an Indiegogo campaign?

00:06:01.419 --> 00:06:06.710
Well it takes at least this, this, this, and
this.

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:12.020
I mean it can be different for everybody but
for the people who were working with me they

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:17.110
were like we really have to spend a month
or two at least two months of just producing

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:21.949
content that sets up the campaign in other
words a building an awareness.

00:06:21.949 --> 00:06:23.510
And how are you going to build an awareness?

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:27.840
Are you just going to comment that I’ making
a movie or do you or in our case create a

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:39.509
lot of videos, sort of mini teases and sort
of aspects of the story and also my biography

00:06:39.509 --> 00:06:44.889
as a filmmaker and some of the films I made,
it was a production of two months before we

00:06:44.889 --> 00:06:50.340
even launched the campaign and then it was
all the videos that had to be created for

00:06:50.340 --> 00:07:00.289
the campaign itself and even then there is
no guarantee how much you’re going to get

00:07:00.289 --> 00:07:07.740
but at least I had to put my best foot forward
as best a job as I could producing that stuff.

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:08.740
So that’s what we did.

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:11.069
Film Courage: Do you have a target date?

00:07:11.069 --> 00:07:12.930
Jack: I want to shoot in January.

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:18.539
I want to shoot the film in January and I
think that’s doable.

00:07:18.539 --> 00:07:22.070
And like I said the actors are on board.

00:07:22.070 --> 00:07:27.349
Kevin Corrigan, Lucy Davis and Barry Bostwick
are all on board (Alan Havey just joined the

00:07:27.349 --> 00:07:28.349
cast).

00:07:28.349 --> 00:07:30.521
And that cast will continue to grow.

00:07:30.521 --> 00:07:34.470
And as soon as we are greenlit (fully greenlit)
I know we are making it on this day I know

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:38.500
we are actually going to get bigger names
to come in because there are a lot of animal

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:43.250
loving celebrities in this town and I’m
sure they will want to get involved in the

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:47.770
picture if it’s actually happening and they
only have to come in for a day or two.

00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:50.250
But January is what we’re shooting for.

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:51.689
Film Courage: Here in LA?

00:07:51.689 --> 00:07:54.340
Jack: Yes partly in LA (the movie is set in
LA).

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:58.720
But again like all independent filmmakers
we will just go where the dollar stretches

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:01.490
farthest.

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:09.360
We actually shot the teaser in Oakland and
that’s where a lot of technicians and people

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:12.770
and I had some infrastructure there.

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:17.870
So we may actually shoot a chunk of it in
Oakland (in the Bay Area) and here.

00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:21.910
But you know, it’s a small budget so you
want to stretch the money.

00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:24.190
Film Courage: Sure.

00:08:24.190 --> 00:08:31.120
And so the crowdfunding campaign most of it
goes to production or will some of it go to

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:32.120
post?

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:38.060
Jack: Well it’s all toward production, just
the means of making the movie.

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:44.320
And I just have to think about if we get enough
to get it in the can, then that’s what we’ll

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:45.320
do.

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:49.760
Then we’ll put it in the can and then use
that material to (if we have to) raise the

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:51.280
additional money.

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:58.940
But I think that the most important part of
the entire process for anybody is that putting

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:03.600
it into play because there is every reason
because there is a million reasons to not

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:04.600
do it.

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:09.900
For most people it is just the fear of it
not happening (or maybe it won’t happen)

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:18.910
and the second I committed to the idea but
that itself (and this is not a new concept)

00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:25.460
but that in and of itself (that mindset) attracted
people to the project and it started to get

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:33.150
its own momentum going and building bit by
bit, person by person, dollar by dollar.

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:34.340
You just keep going.

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:38.960
So I was very grateful that I was just able
to put it into play.

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:42.860
There’s a dog [referring to dog barking],
the dog agrees.

